This document provides comprehensive guidance on React Native and Expo patterns for the One Percent Better mobile application.

## React Native Component Patterns

### Component Structure

- Use React Native components (View, Text, ScrollView, etc.) instead of HTML elements
- Use appropriate components for the use case
- Keep components focused and reusable

```typescript
// Good - React Native component
import { View, Text, TouchableOpacity, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

interface TaskCardProps {
	task: Task;
	onComplete: (taskId: string) => void;
}

export default function TaskCard({ task, onComplete }: TaskCardProps) {
	return (
		<View style={styles.container}>
			<Text style={styles.title}>{task.title}</Text>
			<TouchableOpacity onPress={() => onComplete(task._id.toString())} style={styles.button}>
				<Text>Complete</Text>
			</TouchableOpacity>
		</View>
	);
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
	container: {
		padding: 16,
		backgroundColor: '#fff',
	},
	title: {
		fontSize: 18,
		fontWeight: 'bold',
	},
	button: {
		backgroundColor: '#007AFF',
		padding: 12,
		borderRadius: 8,
	},
});
```

### Common React Native Components

- **View**: Container component (replaces `div`)
- **Text**: Text content (replaces `span`, `p`, etc.)
- **ScrollView**: Scrollable container
- **FlatList**: Efficient list rendering
- **TouchableOpacity**: Button with opacity feedback
- **Pressable**: Modern pressable component
- **TextInput**: Text input field
- **Image**: Image display
- **ActivityIndicator**: Loading spinner

## Styling with StyleSheet

### StyleSheet API

- Always use `StyleSheet.create()` for styles
- Define styles at the bottom of the component file
- Use consistent spacing, colors, and typography
- Avoid inline styles (except for dynamic values)

```typescript
// Good - Using StyleSheet
import { StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
	container: {
		flex: 1,
		padding: 16,
		backgroundColor: '#fff',
	},
	title: {
		fontSize: 24,
		fontWeight: 'bold',
		marginBottom: 8,
	},
	button: {
		backgroundColor: '#007AFF',
		padding: 12,
		borderRadius: 8,
		alignItems: 'center',
	},
});

// Good - Dynamic styles
<View style={[styles.container, { backgroundColor: isActive ? '#fff' : '#f0f0f0' }]} />

// Bad - Inline styles for static values
<View style={{ padding: 16, backgroundColor: '#fff' }} />
```

### Style Best Practices

- Use flexbox for layouts (`flex`, `flexDirection`, `justifyContent`, `alignItems`)
- Use consistent spacing units (8, 16, 24, etc.)
- Extract colors to constants
- Use platform-specific styles when needed

```typescript
// Good - Extracted constants
const COLORS = {
	primary: '#007AFF',
	background: '#fff',
	text: '#000',
};

const SPACING = {
	small: 8,
	medium: 16,
	large: 24,
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
	container: {
		padding: SPACING.medium,
		backgroundColor: COLORS.background,
	},
});
```

## React Navigation

### Navigation Setup

- Use React Navigation for screen navigation
- Define navigation param lists with TypeScript
- Type navigation props properly

```typescript
// Good - Navigation setup
import { NavigationContainer } from '@react-navigation/native';
import { createStackNavigator } from '@react-navigation/stack';

export type RootStackParamList = {
	Home: undefined;
	CreateTask: { taskId?: string };
	Statistics: undefined;
	Settings: undefined;
};

const Stack = createStackNavigator<RootStackParamList>();

export default function App() {
	return (
		<NavigationContainer>
			<Stack.Navigator>
				<Stack.Screen name="Home" component={HomeScreen} />
				<Stack.Screen name="CreateTask" component={CreateTaskScreen} />
				<Stack.Screen name="Statistics" component={StatisticsScreen} />
				<Stack.Screen name="Settings" component={SettingsScreen} />
			</Stack.Navigator>
		</NavigationContainer>
	);
}
```

### Navigation Usage

- Use typed navigation hooks
- Navigate with typed params
- Handle navigation events

```typescript
// Good - Typed navigation
import { useNavigation } from '@react-navigation/native';
import type { NativeStackNavigationProp } from '@react-navigation/native-stack';

type NavigationProp = NativeStackNavigationProp<RootStackParamList>;

export default function HomeScreen() {
	const navigation = useNavigation<NavigationProp>();

	const handleCreateTask = () => {
		navigation.navigate('CreateTask');
	};

	const handleEditTask = (taskId: string) => {
		navigation.navigate('CreateTask', { taskId });
	};

	return (
		// ...
	);
}
```

## Expo Notifications

### Notification Setup

- Request permissions on app start
- Configure notification handler
- Handle notification interactions

```typescript
// Good - Notification setup
import * as Notifications from 'expo-notifications';

Notifications.setNotificationHandler({
	handleNotification: async () => ({
		shouldShowAlert: true,
		shouldPlaySound: true,
		shouldSetBadge: true,
	}),
});

export async function requestNotificationPermissions(): Promise<boolean> {
	const { status: existingStatus } = await Notifications.getPermissionsAsync();
	let finalStatus = existingStatus;

	if (existingStatus !== 'granted') {
		const { status } = await Notifications.requestPermissionsAsync();
		finalStatus = status;
	}

	return finalStatus === 'granted';
}
```

### Scheduling Notifications

- Schedule notifications for tasks
- Use unique identifiers tied to task IDs
- Handle notification cancellation

```typescript
// Good - Scheduling notifications
export async function scheduleTaskNotification(task: Task): Promise<string> {
	const notificationId = await Notifications.scheduleNotificationAsync({
		content: {
			title: task.title,
			body: task.action,
			data: { taskId: task._id.toString() },
		},
		trigger: {
			date: getNotificationDate(task),
		},
	});

	return notificationId;
}

export async function cancelTaskNotification(notificationId: string): Promise<void> {
	await Notifications.cancelScheduledNotificationAsync(notificationId);
}
```

### Handling Notification Interactions

- Handle notification taps
- Handle notification actions (Complete, Skip, Snooze)
- Navigate to appropriate screen

```typescript
// Good - Handling notification interactions
useEffect(() => {
	const subscription = Notifications.addNotificationResponseReceivedListener(response => {
		const taskId = response.notification.request.content.data?.taskId;
		if (taskId) {
			navigation.navigate('CreateTask', { taskId });
		}
	});

	return () => subscription.remove();
}, [navigation]);
```

## Performance Optimization

### List Rendering

- Use `FlatList` for long lists
- Provide `keyExtractor` and `getItemLayout` when possible
- Use `React.memo` for list items

```typescript
// Good - Optimized FlatList
<FlatList
	data={tasks}
	keyExtractor={(item) => item._id.toString()}
	renderItem={({ item }) => <TaskCard task={item} />}
	getItemLayout={(data, index) => ({
		length: ITEM_HEIGHT,
		offset: ITEM_HEIGHT * index,
		index,
	})}
/>
```

### Memoization

- Use `useMemo` for expensive computations
- Use `useCallback` for stable function references
- Use `React.memo` for components with stable props

```typescript
// Good - Memoization
const filteredTasks = useMemo(() => {
	return tasks.filter(task => task.isActive);
}, [tasks]);

const handleComplete = useCallback((taskId: string) => {
	completeTask(taskId);
}, []);

const MemoizedTaskCard = React.memo(TaskCard);
```

### Image Optimization

- Use appropriate image formats
- Optimize image sizes
- Use `resizeMode` appropriately

```typescript
// Good - Image optimization
<Image
	source={{ uri: imageUrl }}
	style={styles.image}
	resizeMode="cover"
/>
```

## Platform-Specific Code

### Platform Detection

- Use `Platform.OS` for platform detection
- Use platform-specific styles when needed
- Use platform-specific components

```typescript
// Good - Platform-specific code
import { Platform, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
	container: {
		padding: 16,
		...Platform.select({
			ios: {
				shadowColor: '#000',
				shadowOffset: { width: 0, height: 2 },
				shadowOpacity: 0.25,
				shadowRadius: 3.84,
			},
			android: {
				elevation: 5,
			},
		}),
	},
});
```

## Accessibility

### Accessibility Props

- Add `accessibilityLabel` for screen readers
- Use `accessibilityRole` for semantic meaning
- Provide `accessibilityHint` for complex interactions

```typescript
// Good - Accessibility
<TouchableOpacity
	accessibilityLabel="Complete task"
	accessibilityRole="button"
	accessibilityHint="Marks this task as completed"
	onPress={handleComplete}
>
	<Text>Complete</Text>
</TouchableOpacity>
```

### Touch Targets

- Ensure touch targets are at least 44x44 points (iOS) or 48x48 dp (Android)
- Provide adequate spacing between interactive elements

```typescript
// Good - Adequate touch target
const styles = StyleSheet.create({
	button: {
		minHeight: 44, // iOS minimum
		minWidth: 44,
		padding: 12,
	},
});
```

## Error Handling

### Error Boundaries

- Use error boundaries for React Native components
- Handle errors gracefully
- Show user-friendly error messages

```typescript
// Good - Error handling
try {
	await scheduleNotification(task);
} catch (error) {
	if (error instanceof Error) {
		Alert.alert('Error', `Failed to schedule notification: ${error.message}`);
	}
}
```

## Best Practices Summary

1. **Components**: Use React Native components, keep focused and reusable
2. **Styling**: Use StyleSheet API, extract constants, use flexbox
3. **Navigation**: Define typed param lists, use typed navigation hooks
4. **Notifications**: Request permissions, schedule with unique IDs, handle interactions
5. **Performance**: Use FlatList, memoize expensive operations, optimize images
6. **Platform**: Use Platform.OS for detection, platform-specific styles/components
7. **Accessibility**: Add accessibility props, ensure adequate touch targets
8. **Error Handling**: Handle errors gracefully, show user-friendly messages
